# Skills Storage Workflow

## Purpose
Keep skill definitions consistent across master source and global agent directories.

## Trigger
Apply when user asks to create, update, or sync a skill.

## Mandatory Clarification Step
Before creating or editing a skill, ask and confirm:
1. Target location
2. Skill folder name
3. Agent profile scope (`Cursor`, `Codex`, `Zed`, `OpenCode`, or all)

## Source of Truth
- Shipped skill: `src/gdskills/bundled/skills/<category>/<skill-name>/` in the
  keryx repository. `.metaproject/skills/gdskills/<category>/<skill-name>/`
  is an installed mirror, regenerated by `keryx init`/`keryx update` — never
  edit it directly, edits there are overwritten on the next run.
- Project-local skill: `.metaproject/project-skills/<skill-name>/`.

## Required Source Layout
- `SKILL.md` — canonical source of truth, the shared build used by every runtime
- `SKILL.cursor.md` — Cursor-specific variant, present only where Cursor's build genuinely differs from `SKILL.md` (falls back to `SKILL.md` otherwise)
- `SKILL.codex.md` — Codex-specific variant, present only where Codex's build genuinely differs from `SKILL.md` (falls back to `SKILL.md` otherwise)
- `SKILL.zed.md` — Zed-specific variant, present only where Zed's build genuinely differs from `SKILL.md` (falls back to `SKILL.md` otherwise)
- `SKILL.opencode.md` — OpenCode-specific variant, present only where OpenCode's build genuinely differs from `SKILL.md` (falls back to `SKILL.md` otherwise)

`SKILL.md` is always required. A `SKILL.<runtime>.md` variant is the
exception, not the norm: create one only when that runtime's build genuinely
differs from `SKILL.md`, and never add one as an identical copy. Falling
back to `SKILL.md` is the normal case for every runtime without a variant —
for `keryx update`/`keryx init` (installed mirror) and for `keryx skills
export`/`keryx skills sync` (global destinations) alike — not a degraded
path.

"Genuinely differs" covers two cases, and only two:

1. **Different instructions.** The runtime needs prose `SKILL.md` does not
   carry, or must not carry.
2. **A different `metadata.compatible_harnesses`.** That field is the
   machine-readable CSV of supported harnesses, so its correct value is
   per-build by construction — a build cannot share a harness list that
   excludes the harness reading it. This is the only field in the tree of
   which that is true.

Case 2 is why fourteen shipped builds differ from their `SKILL.md` by exactly
one line: the seven `gproject-*` subagent skills under `planning/`
(`project-discovery`, `problem-definer`, `patterns-researcher`, `planner`,
`consistency-checker`, `spec-writer`, `stack-advisor`) each ship a
`SKILL.codex.md` and a `SKILL.cursor.md` that add
`compatible_harnesses: "cursor,codex,zed,opencode"`. Their `SKILL.md` declares
no harness list, because it is the Claude build and cannot carry one that
excludes Claude. These builds are NOT identical copies and must not be deleted
as such; `src/gdskills/build-parity.test.ts` exempts this one hunk, by name,
for these seven skills only, and its allow-list is where any widening has to be
argued. Every other difference between a build and its `SKILL.md` is drift and
gets reconciled.

## Frontmatter Fields (Agent Skills spec alignment)

`SKILL.md` frontmatter follows the published Agent Skills specification
(agentskills.io — the format Zed adopted when it dropped its own Rules
Library), with two deliberate additions. Flow 203 removed two accidental
divergences that had crept in; keep both fixes intact when authoring or
editing a skill:

- **`version` lives in `metadata.version` only.** The spec has no top-level
  `version` field — its own example nests it under `metadata`. Do not
  reintroduce a top-level `version:` key; nothing in this codebase reads one.
- **`compatibility` carries the spec's meaning, not ours.** The spec defines
  `compatibility` as environment requirements written in prose (e.g. "Requires
  git, docker, jq, and access to the internet"). Our machine-readable CSV of
  supported harnesses (`cursor,codex,zed,opencode,claude`) lives instead at
  `metadata.compatible_harnesses`. Do not put a harness CSV back into
  `compatibility` — if a skill genuinely needs to declare environment
  requirements, write them there as prose, per the spec.

Two divergences are deliberate and stay — a later pass must not "fix" them
back toward the spec:

- **`triggers`** — a list of trigger phrases. Not in the spec, and absent from
  every skill collection surveyed while building this convention; the spec's
  only discovery mechanism is semantic matching on `description`. `triggers`
  gives the router a cheap, deterministic first-pass match before falling back
  to semantic matching. It is additive and costs nothing as long as
  `description` stays self-sufficient on its own — never let `triggers` carry
  meaning `description` lacks.
- **Per-skill `input-contract.schema.json` / `output-contract.schema.json`**
  (sibling files, not frontmatter keys) — genuinely unique to this codebase.
  Nothing in the spec or any surveyed collection defines one, because the spec
  assumes a single agent reading a single skill file. This project dispatches
  typed payloads to subagent workers instead, which the spec does not attempt
  to address.

## Length Ceilings

Every shipped skill has a line-count ceiling recorded in
`src/gdskills/skill-length-ceilings.ts`, set to the skill's length on the day
the file was introduced; a skill with no entry is bounded by the default (500
lines, the point at which a skill should be split). `keryx skills verify
--bundled` reports `anatomy:length` when a skill passes its ceiling.

A ceiling **moves down**, never up:

- trimming a skill — moving reference material into a sibling document, cutting
  a section that no longer changes behaviour — lowers the ceiling to the new
  count in the same change;
- a skill that needs more room than its ceiling allows is split, or its
  reference material moves out. Raising the number to clear the finding is the
  one edit this rule forbids: it converts a measured limit into a record of
  whatever the file grew to.

`keryx skills verify --bundled` states the same remedy in the same words, and
cites this section by name, so the finding and the rule cannot drift apart. The
`anatomy:length` message reads, after the count:

> Split the skill, or move reference material into a sibling document, and lower
> the ceiling to the new count in the same change. A ceiling only ever moves
> DOWN: raising it is the one edit rules/core/skills-storage-workflow.mdc
> ("Length Ceilings") forbids, because it converts a measured limit into a record
> of whatever the file grew to.

Changing either text without the other is the drift this quotation exists to
prevent; `src/gdskills/bundled-eval.ts` is where the message lives.

A ceiling is not a statement that the skill's current size is right — several
shipped skills are far past the default and were recorded as they stood. It
bounds unnoticed growth, which is the part no other check can see.

Editing a skill is therefore a two-file change. Every recorded ceiling equals
that skill's current count today, so adding a sentence needs a compensating
deletion or a split: recount the file (`wc -l SKILL.md`) and write that number
to the skill's key in `src/gdskills/skill-length-ceilings.ts` in the SAME
commit. A new skill needs a new entry; a deleted skill needs its entry removed.
`keryx skills verify --bundled` only reports `lines > ceiling`, so a TRIM that
leaves the old, higher number behind passes locally and fails the
ceiling-equals-count check in CI.

## Global Sync Mapping
Global sync only ever reaches a **project-local skill**
(`.metaproject/project-skills/<skill-name>/`) that has first been exported for
that runtime with `keryx skills export <skill-name> --runtime <runtime>`
(this writes `.metaproject/runtime/skills/<runtime>/<module>-<skill-name>/`).
A **shipped** skill (`src/gdskills/bundled/skills/<category>/<skill-name>/`)
cannot be exported — `keryx skills export` resolves project skills only and
reports `Project skill not found for: <name>` for a shipped one — so it has no
global-sync route today. A shipped skill reaches a runtime only through the
project-local installed mirror (`.metaproject/skills/gdskills/<category>/<skill-name>/`,
written by `keryx init`/`keryx update`), which stays inside the project.

Once a project skill is exported for a runtime, `keryx skills sync --runtime
<runtime> --global` copies it from `.metaproject/runtime/skills/<runtime>/` to:
- `cursor` → `~/.cursor/skills/<module>-<skill-name>/SKILL.md` (built from `SKILL.cursor.md` when the skill ships one, otherwise `SKILL.md`)
- `codex` → `${CODEX_HOME:-~/.codex}/skills/<module>-<skill-name>/SKILL.md` (built from `SKILL.codex.md` when the skill ships one, otherwise `SKILL.md`)
- `zed` → `~/.config/zed/skills/<module>-<skill-name>/SKILL.md` (built from `SKILL.zed.md` when the skill ships one, otherwise `SKILL.md`)
- `opencode` → `~/.config/opencode/skills/<module>-<skill-name>/SKILL.md` (built from `SKILL.opencode.md` when the skill ships one, otherwise `SKILL.md`)

These four global destinations are written only by the explicit, opt-in
`keryx skills sync --runtime <runtime> --global` command — never as a side
effect of `keryx update`/`keryx init`. `claude` and `plugin` have no global
destination; use `keryx skills sync --runtime <runtime> --target <dir>` for
those instead.

## Mandatory Behavior
1. Always create/update `SKILL.md` as the canonical source.
2. Create a `SKILL.<runtime>.md` only when that runtime's build genuinely differs from `SKILL.md` — either in its instructions or in its `metadata.compatible_harnesses`, the two cases named under Required Source Layout — never as an identical copy. Otherwise `SKILL.md` serves that runtime via fallback; that fallback is the normal case, not an exception.
3. Keep all profiles aligned in structure and intent.
4. Validate skills before sync.
5. For a **project-local** skill only: export it per runtime first (`keryx skills export <skill-name> --runtime <runtime>`), then sync every global destination that applies (`cursor`, `codex`, `zed`, `opencode` — four in total) with `keryx skills sync --runtime <runtime> --global`, run once per runtime that needs it. A shipped skill cannot be exported and has no global-sync route — it reaches a runtime only via the project-local installed mirror (`keryx update`/`keryx init`).
6. Ensure `agents/openai.yaml` exists where required by the target agent UI.
7. For create/update/sync workflows, produce a machine-readable final result for orchestrators.

## Machine-Readable Contract
No canonical schema file exists for this result yet. Contract requirements:
- One top-level JSON object only.
- Stable required keys.
- Closed objects (`additionalProperties: false`).
- `contract_version` in semver.
- `timestamp_utc` in RFC 3339 UTC.

### Decision Mapping for Orchestrator
- `status=needs_input` -> `decision=ask_clarification`
- `status=ready_for_implementation` -> `decision=proceed_to_implementation`
- `status=implemented` -> `decision=proceed_to_sync` or `stop`
- `status=blocked|failed` -> `decision=stop`

## Pre-Sync Validation (Mandatory)
Run before syncing skills:

```bash
keryx skills verify --all
```

Then regenerate the project-local installed mirror
(`.metaproject/skills/gdskills/<category>/<skill-name>/`) from the bundled
source:

```bash
keryx update
```

`keryx update` never writes outside the project, and it is the only route a
**shipped** skill has to reach a runtime — a shipped skill cannot be exported
and has no global-sync destination.

To push a **project-local** skill (`.metaproject/project-skills/<skill-name>/`)
to a harness's own global directory (opt-in, outside the project), export it
for that runtime first, then sync:

```bash
keryx skills export <skill-name> --runtime <runtime>
keryx skills sync --runtime <runtime> --global
```

If validation fails, do not sync.

## Naming
- `skill-name` must be lowercase with numbers/hyphens only.
- Keep names semantic and stable.

## Forbidden
- Do not create skills in `~/.cursor/skills-cursor/`.

## Rejected Changes
Scope: keryx's own bundled skills and rules
(`src/gdskills/bundled/**` in the keryx repository) only. This rule ships to
every project via `keryx init`/`keryx update`, but the ledger below exists
only in the keryx source repo — this section does not apply to
project-local skills (`.metaproject/project-skills/<skill-name>/`).

- Before proposing a change to a shipped skill or rule, search
  `docs/skills/rejected-skill-changes.md` in the keryx repository for that
  skill and that idea.
- When a change to a shipped skill or rule is rejected (review, a
  routing/behaviour-eval regression, or a user/maintainer decision), append a
  row to that ledger — in the same change, on the default branch (`main`) —
  recording what was tried, why it was rejected, and the evidence (e.g. a
  routing-eval rank or behaviour-eval number, before → after). Never edit or
  delete an existing row.

## Apply Changes Workflow
Edit skills under `src/gdskills/bundled/skills/<category>/<skill-name>/`
(shipped) or `.metaproject/project-skills/<skill-name>/` (project-local).
`keryx init`/`keryx update` regenerate the installed mirror at
`.metaproject/skills/gdskills/<category>/<skill-name>/` — this stays inside
the project, and is the only way a shipped skill reaches a runtime. For a
project-local skill only, run `keryx skills export <skill-name> --runtime
<runtime>` then `keryx skills sync --runtime <runtime> --global` separately to
push it to a harness's own global directory outside the project; shipped
skills cannot be exported (`keryx skills export` resolves project skills
only) and have no global-sync route.
